WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
BIG TECH SALARIES:
Over the past decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:06.440
tech salaries have gone to the moon. Thanks&nbsp;
to high profit margins, ubiquitous scale and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:12.160
seemingly infinite growth potential, there was&nbsp;
basically no limit when it came to compensation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.560
It was almost like NFL recruiting or something.&nbsp;
Engineers would get offers from multiple top tier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.240
companies and have them enter a bidding war&nbsp;
against each other. And over time, this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.880
resulted in entry level engineers earning nearly&nbsp;
$200,000, senior managers crossing $400,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.040
and directors earning more than a million dollars&nbsp;
per year. And given that these compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.080
packages often came with a bunch of stock that&nbsp;
was growing 5-10x if not more, we saw the rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:45.360
of a whole new demographic of multi millionaires&nbsp;
and deca millionaires. All of this peaked in 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.480
when demand for top tier engineers went through&nbsp;
the roof thanks to the pandemic. Tech compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.440
was growing faster than real estate prices,&nbsp;
and one point, Zuckerberg was even throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.320
a tantrum about how only half of his top tier&nbsp;
job offers were being accepted. But a lot has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.720
changed since then and it seems that this decade&nbsp;
of seemingly infinite compensation growth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.200
finally coming to an end. With high interest rates&nbsp;
and stagnating revenue growth, tech companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.720
finally having to shift their focus to maximizing&nbsp;
profits. This included new monetization efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.280
layoffs, eliminating unprofitable programs,&nbsp;
and of course cutting salaries. But this wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.280
as straightforward as you might think. It’s not&nbsp;
like Google is suddenly handing out compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.000
packages that are a good amount lower than usual.&nbsp;
This would not only make it hard to recruit new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.080
engineers but it would also earn them a bunch&nbsp;
of bad press in the one area that they’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.640
highly respected: strong compensation and great&nbsp;
corporate culture. As such, these companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:49.120
decided to go about salary cuts in a much more&nbsp;
nefarious manner, hiding them in fine print and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:59.840
seemingly pro employee policies. So, here’s&nbsp;
the overdue collapse of big tech salaries.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.480
PREDATORY VESTING:
To really understand how exactly these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.800
companies are cutting compensation, we have to&nbsp;
first understand how big tech compensation works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.040
For example, if you’re a director at Facebook,&nbsp;
it’s not like you’re earning $1.3 million per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.240
year cash. Rather, only about $320,000 is paid out&nbsp;
in the form of base salary. The vast majority or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:28.240
nearly $900,000 is paid out in the form of stock&nbsp;
compensation. There are some exceptions to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.600
rule like Netflix who truly do pay out million&nbsp;
dollar cash salaries. But for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.800
a more modest cash salary plus an insane stock&nbsp;
package is how big tech compensation breaks down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.440
And the recent pay cuts primarily have to do with&nbsp;
the stock portion of the compensation. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.960
the stock portion isn’t exactly what it seems&nbsp;
either. It’s not like you’re consistently paid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.240
stock grant every month just like a cash salary.&nbsp;
Rather, the stock grant usually comes in blocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.320
of 4 years and unlocks in regular intervals&nbsp;
throughout that time period. For example, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.680
an engineer was earning an average of a $100,000&nbsp;
per year in stock, then their actual compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:12.360
package was probably $400,000 worth of stock&nbsp;
over 4 years. And after the 4 years is up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.080
they might get refreshers or they might need&nbsp;
to get promoted in order to get another 4 year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:22.880
grant. Now, all of that might seem like too many&nbsp;
details but it’s important because like I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.680
the catch is in the fine print. The industry&nbsp;
standard is for stock grants to unlock in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:34.000
consistent manner throughout the vesting period&nbsp;
at 25% per year. Anything else is sus and there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.480
usually more going on than what meets the eye.&nbsp;
Amazon for example is notorious for sporting an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.400
extremely skewed vesting schedule. In year 1,&nbsp;
only 5% of the stock grant unlocks and in year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:52.880
2 only 15% unlocks. It’s not till year 3 and 4&nbsp;
that you receive 80% of your stock grant. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.840
do they do this you ask? Well, Amazon knows&nbsp;
that the average employee doesn’t even last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.000
2 years meaning that they likely wont even&nbsp;
unlock their 15% for year 2. So, when hiring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.160
Amazon is able to pitch a $400,000 stock grant as&nbsp;
an average of $100,000 per year in compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.600
but the average employee only ends up unlocking 5%&nbsp;
of this or $20,000, saving Amazon a bunch of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:22.640
in terms of stock comp. Coming from Amazon,&nbsp;
that’s probably not all that surprising but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.120
backloaded stock vesting is just one trick in the&nbsp;
book. Another trick that companies use is annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.560
vesting like is the case with Stripe. Instead of&nbsp;
receiving a 4 year grant that unlocks over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.080
Stripe likes to hand out a new 1 year grant every&nbsp;
year which makes it much harder for employee stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:44.360
comp to compound with the company. For example,&nbsp;
if you were earning $100,000 per year in stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.200
and your company triples over the next 4 years,&nbsp;
then you’d be earning $300,000 in the 4th year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:55.040
At Stripe, however, you’d still be earning&nbsp;
$100,000 despite potentially playing a crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.080
role in helping the company triple. These sort&nbsp;
of vesting shenanigans have always been common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:12.499
throughout tech but recently, we saw Google&nbsp;
stoop to this tier as well with their own trick.

00:05:12.499 --> 00:05:15.440
FRONT LOADED VESTING:
In 2021, Google adopted a new front loaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.600
vesting schedule which consisted of 33% in the&nbsp;
first 2 years, 22% in the 3rd year, and 12% in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.880
4th. At first glance, it seems like this vesting&nbsp;
schedule is actually more generous. They’re giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:35.240
employees more of the stock grant up front but&nbsp;
don’t be fooled, that’s by design. You see, if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:41.880
overall stock grant stayed consistent, this would&nbsp;
indeed be a superior vesting plan. But that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.880
quite what Google is doing. Rather, they’re using&nbsp;
front loaded vesting to inflate their 1st year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.400
compensation numbers. For example, if you were&nbsp;
a potential L6 hire at Google, they might tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:59.920
you this. We’ll pay you industry standard 1st year&nbsp;
comp of $250 cash, $250 stock, and $50 bonus. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:06.040
sounds about right but the catch is in the stock.&nbsp;
Before, the $250 stock that you would earn in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:11.080
first year was only a quarter of the total stock&nbsp;
you would receive. Aka, you would earn a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:16.560
a million dollars in stock over the next 4 years.&nbsp;
But now, the $250 stock that you earn in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:23.120
year is a third of your total stock comp. Aka, you&nbsp;
now only earn $750 in stock over the next 4 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.280
and Google saves a full quarter million. And,&nbsp;
that was just with their original front loaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:33.960
vesting plan. Since then, Google has become even&nbsp;
more aggressive with their front loaded vesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:40.360
Their current main vesting plan is 38% in the 1st&nbsp;
year, 32% in the 2nd year, 20% in the 3rd year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:46.000
and 10% in the 4th year. Again, if you were an&nbsp;
L6 engineer, this means that instead of earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:53.080
$1 million over 4 years, you would now “only”&nbsp;
earn $657,000. And it doesn’t stop right there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:58.760
either. Google has an even more aggressive front&nbsp;
loaded plan as well. In this vesting schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:06.840
you get 50% in the 1st year, 28% in the 2nd year,&nbsp;
12% in the 3rd year, and 10% in the 4th year. Aka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.760
instead of earning a million in stock,&nbsp;
you’re now down to half a million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:15.600
and Google has effectively saved on nearly a full&nbsp;
year of compensation that they would’ve previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:22.040
paid out. Obviously quite a bit of savings but&nbsp;
all of that only tells half the story. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:28.320
the main benefits of stock compensation is that it&nbsp;
can enjoy outsized returns as the company grows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.800
But as big tech approaches maturity, the expected&nbsp;
returns on stock comp also becomes a lot lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:39.320
due to diminishing returns. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;
it’s much easier to go from being a $250 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.920
company to a $1 trillion company than it is to go&nbsp;
from $1 trillion to $4 trillion, and this plays a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:51.080
huge role in total comp. If you joined Google&nbsp;
in 2017 for example, you saw Google stock over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:56.080
triple. This meant that the $1 million stock grant&nbsp;
that you got when you joined was actually worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.520
$3 million by the time it fully unlocked. If you&nbsp;
joined Google in 2021, however, you were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.600
down as much as 45% on your stock. But let’s even&nbsp;
assume that Google ends up fully recovering and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:13.520
growing by 50% over the next 2 years. This would&nbsp;
be phenomenal for a mature company like Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:20.600
but the same cannot be said about comp. You’re now&nbsp;
half as large stock grant of $500,000 will “only”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:27.120
end up being $750 at the end of 4 years. In other&nbsp;
words, it’s now 3-4 times less lucrative to join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.040
Google today than it was just a couple of years&nbsp;
ago. And all of that is assuming that you’re able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:44.342
to get the same 1st year comp. It turns out that&nbsp;
that itself would probably be quite challenging.

00:08:44.342 --> 00:08:46.520
A SALARY COLLAPSE:
Everything that we’ve talked about so far are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:52.000
shadow cuts: things that aren’t really apparent&nbsp;
unless you look at the fine print. But, the truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.760
is that big tech has had upfront salary cuts as&nbsp;
well. Thanks to remote work, big tech has not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.280
had an opportunity to pay less for office space&nbsp;
but pay employees less as well. In fact, Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:09.480
directly cut salaries of remote workers by as&nbsp;
much as 25%. Now, this isn’t as bad as it sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.040
because these cuts were in line with employees&nbsp;
moving to lower cost of living cities. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:19.240
this is nonetheless massive salary savings for&nbsp;
Google. Big tech has in general started focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.360
their hiring efforts on lower cost of living&nbsp;
areas within the US and offshoring many jobs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:29.440
sectors. Google has also cut a bunch of employee&nbsp;
benefits and even gone as far as tracking employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:35.080
badges to maximize productivity. I’ve been picking&nbsp;
on Google for most of this video but that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:40.480
because they’re the most notable example. These&nbsp;
same practices have been commonplace across all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.400
of the tech. And if you were to confront these&nbsp;
companies about any of this, they’d probably have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:50.520
a couple of key counterpoints. They might say&nbsp;
that the overall stock grant doesn’t decrease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:56.240
by as much as the vesting schedule might suggest.&nbsp;
For example, 50% vesting in year 1 would suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:03.080
a stock comp cut of as much as 50% but the real&nbsp;
number might actually only be 20 or 30%. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:07.640
would probably also bring up a point about how&nbsp;
they hand out stock refreshers in year 3 and 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:13.200
that could help make up for any decreases in total&nbsp;
comp. But regardless of how exactly they justify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.800
it, it doesn’t really change that fact that&nbsp;
working in big tech isn’t nearly as lucrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:23.440
as it once used to be. Back then, your total&nbsp;
stock comp wouldn’t have been 20 to 30% less,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.280
you would’ve still gotten the same refreshers in&nbsp;
year 3 and 4, you wouldn’t be facing salary cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.840
due to remote work, and the stock would’ve had&nbsp;
substantially more upside potential. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:36.880
you put all these factors together, you’ll&nbsp;
see that joining FAANG today could easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:44.560
be 50 to 70% less lucrative than it was in the&nbsp;
2010s. With that being said, it’s not like tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:50.480
workers are doing bad by any means. Instead of a&nbsp;
staff engineer earning $4 million over 4 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.520
now they’re $1.5 to 2 million which is indeed&nbsp;
substantially less but also still an insane amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:01.960
of compensation that’s a lot higher than you could&nbsp;
earn anywhere else. So, I’m not exactly suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:07.160
that you feel bad for FAANG engineers but it&nbsp;
does bring up the question of: where exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:13.040
does this leave big tech? Well, all of these cost&nbsp;
saving measures are only now going into place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:18.080
so expect it to take 4-5 years for all of the&nbsp;
results to come it, but it’s likely that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:23.600
operating profit per employee at these companies&nbsp;
will go up substantially. This will be inline with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.280
these companies honing in on their final push for&nbsp;
monetization as well, so bottom lines should go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:34.440
up quite nicely across the board. But that should&nbsp;
pretty much be it as that would result in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:40.000
companies fully maturing. They’ve spent the last&nbsp;
20 years on pure growth which is now very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.600
starting to slow down. So, the logical move is&nbsp;
to focus on maximum profitability and reach their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:52.440
final form and become the next Cisco or IBM. In&nbsp;
the meantime, we’ll likely see the best employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:56.840
at all of these companies leave in droves not&nbsp;
even because of the lower comp but because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:02.480
the work itself. Moving forward, their jobs will&nbsp;
be more about increasing efficiencies and reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.160
overhead as opposed to building the next big&nbsp;
thing. So, it’s only natural that the aspirational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:13.400
types move onto smaller companies where they&nbsp;
can not only earn a lot more but do a lot more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:19.640
And that is the state of big tech compensation&nbsp;
today. All of this is rather new for FAANG but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:24.480
it’s already been playing out in Europe for quite&nbsp;
some time. Check out this video to learn more.

